# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 2 Unit 1 Nature of Contracts
BANK['fnd-p2-ch02-u01'] = [
    Q('c1', 'Under the Indian Contract Act, 1872, "an agreement enforceable by law" is the definition of a:',
      ['Contract — Section 2(h)', 'Agreement — Section 2(e)', 'Promise — Section 2(b)', 'Void agreement — Section 2(g)'],
      'Section 2(h) defines a contract as an agreement enforceable by law. Section 2(e) treats an agreement as a promise, or a set of promises, supported by mutual consideration, and Section 2(g) says an agreement not enforceable by law is void. So every contract is an agreement, but not every agreement is a contract.',
      '"Enforceable by law" is the key phrase of 2(h). Agreement + enforceability = contract.', 30),
    Q('c5', 'A shop displays a saree with a price tag of ₹4,500. Rani picks it up and goes to the counter, but the shopkeeper refuses to sell. Which is correct?',
      ['The display is only an invitation to offer; Rani made the offer, which the shopkeeper may refuse', 'The display is an offer; the shopkeeper is bound to sell', 'Rani accepted the offer by picking up the saree', 'A contract arose when Rani entered the shop'],
      'Goods displayed with price tags are an invitation to offer, not an offer (Pharmaceutical Society of Great Britain v. Boots). The customer makes the offer at the counter and the shopkeeper may accept or reject it. Since no acceptance was given, no contract arose and Rani cannot compel the sale.',
      'Price tags, catalogues, menus, tenders invited = invitation to offer. The customer offers.', 40),
    Q('c6', 'Arun offers to sell his scooter to Bina for ₹60,000. Bina replies, "I accept if you reduce it to ₹55,000." Arun refuses. Bina then says she accepts at ₹60,000. Is there a contract?',
      ['No — Bina\'s reply was a counter-offer that rejected the original offer, so nothing remained to accept', 'Yes — at ₹60,000, since Bina finally accepted', 'Yes — at ₹55,000', 'Yes — at ₹57,500, the average'],
      'Under Section 7 acceptance must be absolute and unqualified. A conditional reply is a counter-offer, and a counter-offer rejects the original offer (Hyde v. Wrench). Arun\'s offer therefore came to an end; Bina\'s later "acceptance" is only a fresh offer that Arun is free to refuse.',
      'Counter-offer kills the original offer. Later "acceptance" = new offer.', 40, 'moderate'),
    Q('c7', 'Under Section 4, when does the communication of an acceptance bind the proposer?',
      ['When the acceptor dispatches it and can no longer recall it', 'When the proposer reads it', 'When the proposer replies to it', 'When the acceptor decides to accept'],
      'Section 4 splits the moment: the proposer is bound once the acceptor posts the acceptance and can no longer recall it; the acceptor is bound only when the proposer actually learns of it. That is why the acceptor can still revoke his acceptance before it reaches the proposer.',
      'Against proposer = posting. Against acceptor = proposer\'s knowledge.', 40, 'moderate'),
    Q('c7', 'Karan posts an offer on 1 May. Leela posts her acceptance on 3 May; it reaches Karan on 6 May. Karan sends a revocation of his offer on 4 May, which reaches Leela on 5 May. Which is correct?',
      ['The revocation is too late: a contract was made on 3 May when Leela posted her acceptance', 'The revocation is valid because it reached Leela before her acceptance reached Karan', 'No contract, since Karan had not received the acceptance', 'The contract is voidable at Karan\'s option'],
      'Section 5 lets the proposer withdraw his offer only until the acceptance binds him. Under Section 4 that happened on 3 May, when Leela posted her letter. A revocation sent on 4 May is therefore too late, and a binding contract exists.',
      'Proposer can revoke only before acceptance is POSTED. Compare the revocation date with the posting date.', 50, 'hard'),
    Q('c4', 'An offer made to the world at large, which can be accepted by anyone who performs its conditions, is a:',
      ['General offer', 'Specific offer', 'Cross offer', 'Counter offer'],
      'A general offer is made to the public at large, such as a reward for returning a lost dog. Under Section 8 anyone who performs the conditions with knowledge of the offer accepts it (Carlill v. Carbolic Smoke Ball Co.). A specific offer is made to a definite person, and cross offers are identical offers that cross each other.',
      'Offer to "anyone/the public" = general offer, accepted by doing the act.', 30),
    Q('c6', 'Ravi finds a lost child and brings him home, not knowing that the father had announced a reward. Later he learns of the reward and claims it. Ravi will:',
      ['Fail — there can be no acceptance of an offer one does not know about', 'Succeed — because he performed the act', 'Succeed — under Section 70 as a quasi contract', 'Get half the reward'],
      'Acceptance needs knowledge of the offer; performance done in ignorance of it is not acceptance (Lalman Shukla v. Gauri Dutt). Ravi did the act before he knew of the reward, so no contract arose. Section 70 does not help him either, because he did not act intending to be paid.',
      'Did he know of the offer when he acted? No knowledge = no acceptance.', 40, 'moderate'),
    Q('c5', 'Which statement is NOT correct?',
      ['Silence of the offeree amounts to acceptance if the offeror says so', 'An offer must be communicated to the offeree', 'A mere statement of the lowest price is not an offer', 'Terms of an offer must be definite'],
      'The offeror cannot turn the offeree\'s silence into acceptance by saying "if I hear nothing, I shall take it as accepted" (Felthouse v. Bindley). The other statements are correct rules: an offer must be communicated, a quotation of price is not an offer (Harvey v. Facey), and the terms must be certain.',
      'Silence ≠ acceptance — the offeror cannot impose it.', 35),
    Q('c3', 'An agreement that was enforceable when made but later becomes unenforceable because of a change in law becomes:',
      ['A void contract — Section 2(j)', 'A voidable contract', 'An illegal agreement from the start', 'An unenforceable contract that revives later'],
      'Section 2(j): once a valid contract stops being enforceable, from that moment it is void. The agreement was valid when made, so it was not void from the start; it becomes void later, for example because performance becomes unlawful under a new law.',
      'Valid at birth, unenforceable later = void contract (2(j)).', 35),
]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ch 2 Unit 2 Consideration
BANK['fnd-p2-ch02-u02'] = [
    Q('c1', 'At Mohan\'s request, Neel repairs Mohan\'s fence in March. In April, Mohan promises to pay Neel ₹2,000 for it. Is Neel\'s act good consideration?',
      ['Yes — it was done at Mohan\'s desire, and past consideration is recognised under Section 2(d)', 'No — past consideration is never valid', 'No — the promise came after the act, so it is a gift', 'Yes, but only if the promise is registered'],
      'Section 2(d) recognises an act that "has been done" as consideration, so past consideration is valid in India, provided the act was done at the request of the promisor. Neel repaired the fence because Mohan asked him to, so Mohan\'s later promise to pay is supported by consideration and is enforceable.',
      'Ask two things: done at the promisor\'s request? Then past, present or future consideration all work.', 40, 'moderate'),
    Q('c2', 'Gita gifts her property to her daughter Mala on condition that Mala pays ₹10,000 a year to Gita\'s brother. Mala promises the brother in writing but then refuses to pay. The brother sues. He will:',
      ['Succeed — consideration may move from any other person, and Gita\'s gift is consideration for Mala\'s promise', 'Fail — he gave no consideration himself', 'Fail — he is a stranger to consideration and contract', 'Succeed only if he is a minor'],
      'In India the consideration need not come from the promisee himself; Section 2(d) allows a third person to provide it. Here Gita\'s gift supports Mala\'s promise, and the brother may sue even though he himself gave nothing (Chinnaya v. Ramayya). Indian law, unlike English law, allows a stranger to consideration to sue.',
      'Stranger to CONSIDERATION can sue in India; stranger to CONTRACT generally cannot.', 45, 'moderate'),
    Q('c3', 'Ashok agrees to sell his car worth ₹5,00,000 to Bhanu for ₹50,000. Ashok\'s consent was freely given. The agreement is:',
      ['A valid contract — inadequacy of consideration does not invalidate an agreement freely made', 'Void for inadequate consideration', 'Voidable at Ashok\'s option', 'Void under Section 25'],
      'By Explanation 2 to Section 25, a low price alone does not destroy an agreement if consent was free. Inadequacy may only be taken into account by the court when deciding whether consent was free. With free consent proved, the contract stands.',
      'Consideration need not be adequate — only real. Free consent + low price = valid.', 35),
    Q('c6', 'Which agreement without consideration is valid under Section 25?',
      ['A written and registered promise made out of natural love and affection to a near relative', 'An oral promise to pay a debt that has become time-barred', 'A promise to pay for a service done at the request of a third person', 'A promise by a son to donate ₹1 lakh to a temple, made orally'],
      'Section 25(1) protects a promise between near relatives made out of love and affection, provided it is written and registered. A time-barred debt promise is valid only if in writing and signed (Section 25(3)), so an oral one fails. The other two lack consideration and fit no exception.',
      'Section 25(1) needs ALL of: writing, registration, near relation, love and affection.', 40, 'moderate'),
    Q('c6', 'Deepak owed Esha ₹80,000; the debt became time-barred in 2024. In 2026 Deepak gives Esha a signed written promise to pay ₹50,000. Esha can recover:',
      ['₹50,000', '₹80,000', 'Nil', '₹30,000'],
      'Section 25(3) makes valid a written promise, signed by the debtor or his agent, to pay wholly or in part a debt that the creditor could have enforced but for the law of limitation. The promise is enforceable to the extent promised — ₹50,000 here — not the whole original debt.',
      'Time-barred debt: written + signed promise -> recover only the amount PROMISED.', 40, 'moderate', check='50000'),
    Q('c4', 'The rule that an outsider cannot enforce a contract made between two others is the doctrine of:',
      ['Privity of contract', 'Caveat emptor', 'Quantum meruit', 'Frustration'],
      'Under the privity doctrine, an outsider to a contract has no right to enforce it, even if it was made for his benefit (Tweddle v. Atkinson). Exceptions include a beneficiary under a trust, family settlements, acknowledgement or estoppel, assignment and contracts through an agent.',
      'Who can SUE = privity. Caveat emptor is sale of goods; quantum meruit is a remedy.', 25),
    Q('c5', 'Which person still CANNOT sue, i.e. is NOT covered by an exception to privity?',
      ['A person who merely expects to benefit from a contract between two friends', 'A beneficiary under a trust', 'A person entitled under a family settlement reduced to writing', 'A person to whom the promisor has acknowledged the payment'],
      'Recognised exceptions to privity include beneficiaries under a trust or charge, persons entitled under a family arrangement, cases of acknowledgement or estoppel, assignment and agency. Merely expecting to benefit from someone else\'s contract creates no right to sue; that is the general rule, not an exception.',
      '"NOT an exception": pick the one with no trust, family, acknowledgement or assignment link.', 40, 'moderate'),
    Q('c3', 'Assertion (A): A promise to do what one is already legally bound to do is not good consideration.  Reason (R): What the promisee offers as consideration must go beyond what the law already obliges him to do.',
      ar(0),
      'Both are true and R explains A. Performing an existing legal duty — for example, a witness under summons attending court — adds nothing new, so it cannot support a fresh promise. Consideration must be real and something beyond what the law already requires of the promisee.',
      'R restates the rule that produces A -> option "R explains A".', 45, 'moderate'),
]

